toolsAdded.description:
Run JavaScript that calls other tools. The input is raw JavaScript (not JSON, no code fence), run as an async function body in a QuickJS sandbox: top-level `await` and `return` work. No Node, file system, network, or timers.
- `await tools.<name>({ ...args })` resolves to a string, or an object if the tool's declaration says so, and rejects with an Error on failure. A tool whose declaration returns an object resolves on failure too when it returned that object; check its fields. Calls still running when the script ends are cancelled.
- Optional first line: `// @options: {"max_output_tokens": 10000, "timeout_ms": 60000}`

Globals:
- `text(value)`, `image(dataUrlOrImageBlock)`, `console.log(...)`, and top-level `return` add output; `exit()` ends the script. With several text items, each starts with a `==> text N/M <==` line, and `console` lines follow the other output in one `<console_output>` block. `image()` also saves the image to a temp file and the result names its path.
- `store(key, value)` and `load(key)` keep JSON values across codemode calls.
- `ALL_TOOLS`, `await searchTools(query, { limit?, namespace? })`, `await describeTool(name)`, `await describeNamespace(name)`: find unlisted tools, such as MCP tools.
- `models`: classifiers and image generation. Read /Users/retro9/.nvm/versions/node/v26.3.0/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/codemode.md first.

Nested tools:

### `read`
Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.

- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { read(args: {
  // Maximum number of lines to read
  limit?: number;
  // Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)
  offset?: number;
  // Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)
  path: string;
}): Promise<string | { data: string; mimeType: string; note: string; type: "image"; }>; };
```

### `bash`
Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.

- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { bash(args: {
  // Shell command to execute
  command: string;
  // Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)
  timeout?: number;
}): Promise<{
  exit_code: number;
  // Full output, when truncated
  full_output_path?: string;
  // Combined stdout and stderr, possibly truncated
  output: string;
  truncated: boolean;
  wall_time_seconds: number;
}>; };
```

### `edit`
Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.

- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.
- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { edit(args: {
  // One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead.
  edits: Array<{
    // Replacement text for this targeted edit.
    newText: string;
    // Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call.
    oldText: string;
  }>;
  // Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)
  path: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `write`
Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.

- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { write(args: {
  // Content to write to the file
  content: string;
  // Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)
  path: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `worktree_add`
Create a git worktree for a branch in the worktree folder (<folder>/<repo>-wt/<branch with / as ->). Checks out the local branch if it exists, else tracks a remote branch of that name, else creates the branch from base. With enter: true, also enters it (see worktree_enter).

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { worktree_add(args: {
  // Start point of a new branch. Default: HEAD of the repository's main checkout
  // @minLength 1
  base?: string;
  // Branch name, e.g. feat/login
  // @minLength 1
  branch: string;
  // Also enter the new worktree (like worktree_enter)
  enter?: boolean;
  // Repository folder (relative to the session's working directory, or absolute). Default: the entered worktree's repository, else the one the working directory is in.
  repo?: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `worktree_enter`
Enter an existing worktree (by branch) for the rest of the session: relative paths of read/write/edit/ls/grep/find and every bash command run there, and write/edit outside it are blocked. Only the user can leave it.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { worktree_enter(args: {
  // Branch name, e.g. feat/login
  // @minLength 1
  branch: string;
  // Repository folder (relative to the session's working directory, or absolute). Default: the entered worktree's repository, else the one the working directory is in.
  repo?: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `worktree_remove`
Remove the worktree of a branch. Keeps the branch; fails when the worktree has uncommitted changes or the session is in it.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { worktree_remove(args: {
  // Branch name, e.g. feat/login
  // @minLength 1
  branch: string;
  // Repository folder (relative to the session's working directory, or absolute). Default: the entered worktree's repository, else the one the working directory is in.
  repo?: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `worktree_list`
List the worktrees of every repository in the workspace; * marks the one the session works in.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { worktree_list(args: { [key: string]: unknown; }): Promise<string>; };
```

### `subagent_spawn`
Start a subagent: a separate pi agent that runs one task with a scenario's prompt and tools, in the background. Returns its ID (a3) at once; its result arrives later as a `[subagent a3]` message that starts a new turn. Scenarios: demo, work-request-triage, code-writer, the-sculptor, debugger.

- Use `subagent_spawn` to delegate self-contained tasks that can run in parallel with your own work. Give the task everything it needs: the subagent cannot ask you questions.
- Subagent results arrive by themselves as `[subagent aN]` messages; do not poll or wait for them. Look results up again with `subagent_get` / `subagent_grep`.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { subagent_spawn(args: {
  // Project folder (--project) for the subagent
  project?: string;
  // Scenario the subagent runs
  scenario: "demo" | "work-request-triage" | "code-writer" | "the-sculptor" | "debugger";
  // The complete task: goal, context, constraints, what to report
  task: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `subagent_kill`
Kill a running subagent. It ends with a `killed` result.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { subagent_kill(args: {
  // Agent ID, e.g. a3
  id: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `subagent_restart`
Start a finished subagent's task again in a fresh subagent (same scenario, task and project; new ID).

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { subagent_restart(args: {
  // Agent ID, e.g. a3
  id: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `subagent_get`
Show subagent results by ID: task, summary and structured detail, with numbered lines. A running subagent shows its state. Use `lines` for a range and `maxChars` to see more.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { subagent_get(args: {
  // Agent IDs (at most 10)
  // @minItems 1 @maxItems 10
  ids: Array<string>;
  // Line range, 1-based and inclusive; negative counts from the end
  // @minItems 2 @maxItems 2
  lines?: Array<number>;
  // Characters per result (default 4000)
  // @integer @minimum 100
  maxChars?: number;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `subagent_grep`
Find subagent results by regex over their task, summary and detail, newest first, with line numbers for subagent_get. Case-insensitive unless the pattern has an uppercase letter. `pattern: "."` lists the results.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { subagent_grep(args: {
  // Lines of context around each match (default 0)
  // @integer @minimum 0 @maximum 20
  context?: number;
  // Search only these agents
  // @minItems 1 @maxItems 10
  ids?: Array<string>;
  // JavaScript regex, matched line by line
  pattern: string;
}): Promise<string>; };
```

### `tool_ref_get`
Show earlier tool results by their handle (the N of `[tool-ref #N]`): output, input or both. Output lines are numbered; use `lines` for a range and `maxChars` to see more.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { tool_ref_get(args: {
  // Output line range, 1-based and inclusive, e.g. [1, 80]; negative counts from the end, e.g. [-50, -1]
  // @minItems 2 @maxItems 2
  lines?: Array<number>;
  // Characters per ref (default 4000)
  // @integer @minimum 100
  maxChars?: number;
  // What to show (default: output)
  part?: "output" | "input" | "both";
  // Handles to show, e.g. [12, 14] (at most 10)
  // @minItems 1 @maxItems 10
  refs: Array<number>;
}): Promise<{ results: Array<{
  durationMs?: number;
  endedAt?: string;
  // @integer
  handle: number;
  // Original arguments; null unless part is input or both
  input: { [key: string]: unknown; } | null;
  isError: boolean;
  lines?: {
    // @integer
    from: number;
    // @integer
    to: number;
    // @integer
    total: number;
  };
  // Earlier results this call looked up (scripts)
  lookups?: Array<{ pattern?: string; refs?: Array<number>; tool: string; }>;
  // Whole text output (images as [image]) or the requested lines; null for part input
  output: string | null;
  startedAt?: string;
  toolCallId: string;
  toolName: string;
  // Output cut at 1 MiB; request lines
  truncated?: boolean;
} | {
  error: string;
  // @integer
  handle: number;
}>; }>; };
```

### `tool_ref_grep`
Find earlier tool calls by regex over their input and output, newest first, with line numbers for tool_ref_get. `pattern: ".", in: "input"` lists the calls.

codemode tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { tool_ref_grep(args: {
  // Lines of context around each match (default 0)
  // @integer @minimum 0 @maximum 20
  context?: number;
  // true: only failed calls; false: only successful ones
  errors?: boolean;
  // Where to search (default: both)
  in?: "input" | "output" | "both";
  // JavaScript regex, matched line by line; case-insensitive unless it has an uppercase letter
  pattern: string;
  // Search only these handles (at most 10)
  // @minItems 1 @maxItems 10
  refs?: Array<number>;
  // Only calls of these tools
  tool?: Array<string>;
}): Promise<{
  // Calls with matches, newest first
  calls: Array<{
    // @integer
    handle: number | null;
    isError: boolean;
    matches: Array<{
      // @integer
      line: number;
      text: string;
      where: "input" | "output";
    }>;
    toolName: string;
  }>;
  // Problems with handles given in refs
  notes: Array<string>;
  // Matches left out by the caps
  // @integer
  omitted: number;
}>; };
```

arguments.code:
// intent: spawn three demo subagents for UI testing
const tasks = [
  "UI test 1: briefly say hello and report a one-line summary.",
  "UI test 2: list three fruits and report them.",
  "UI test 3: count from 1 to 5 and report the result.",
];
const r = await Promise.allSettled(tasks.map(task => tools.subagent_spawn({ scenario: "demo", task })));
return r.map(x => x.status === "fulfilled" ? x.value : "ERR: " + x.reason);

// intent: restart aborted demo subagents a1 and a3
const r = await Promise.allSettled(["a1","a3"].map(id => tools.subagent_restart({ id })));
return r.map(x => x.status === "fulfilled" ? x.value : "ERR: " + x.reason);
